.Dd November 12, 2026
.Dt SETLOCALE 3
.Os RavenBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm setlocale
.Nm newlocale
.Nm uselocale
.Nm duplocale
.Nm nl_langinfo
.Nm localeconv
.Nd set or query the program locale
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In locale.h
.Ft char *
.Fn setlocale "int category" "const char *locale"
.Ft locale_t
.Fn newlocale "int mask" "const char *name" "locale_t base"
.Ft locale_t
.Fn uselocale "locale_t locale"
.Ft locale_t
.Fn duplocale "locale_t locale"
.Ft char *
.Fn nl_langinfo "nl_item item"
.Ft struct lconv *
.Fn localeconv void
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The locale family selects the character and formatting conventions used by the C library.
RavenBSD keeps one process-wide C locale and a per-thread locale object; the default is the C
locale.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn setlocale returns the selected name or a null pointer on failure. The locale constructors
return a new object or a null pointer. .Fn nl_langinfo returns a pointer to a static string.
.Fn localeconv returns a pointer to the current formatting table.
.Sh ERRORS
.Er EINVAL is returned for an unknown category. The constructors return a null pointer and set
.Er ENOMEM when the object cannot be allocated.
.Sh STANDARDS
ISO C23 and POSIX.1-2024; ring 0. The personality is the C/BSD set.
.Sh CAVEATS
Only the C, POSIX and C.UTF-8 locale names, and names ending in .UTF-8 or .utf8, select a
UTF-8 locale. Any other name is accepted and maps to C.UTF-8. MB_CUR_MAX is 1 in C and 4 in UTF-8.
See H-3 and C48.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr setlocale 3
.Xr locale 7
